## Design

**New file: `flutter_app/lib/apps/app_storage.dart`** — the shared `AppStorage` namespace helper (I1):

```dart
/// The single app-storage namespace: every `apps/<id>/` path the Apps tab
/// lists, reads, writes, or boots resolves through [appDir]/[appFile] here —
/// one validator, one clamp, no second path normalization (gh-1614 I1).
class AppStorage {
  /// Valid app id: `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,31}$`.
  static final RegExp idPattern = RegExp(r'^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,31}$');
  static const String root = 'apps';
  static bool isValidId(String id) => idPattern.hasMatch(id);
  /// Resolves `apps/<id>` or throws ArgumentFormatException naming the id.
  static String appDir(String id) { validate; return 'apps/$id'; }
  static String appFile(String id, String name) ...
  static String manifestPath(String id) => appFile(id, 'manifest.json');
  static String entryPath(String id, [String entry = 'widget.js']) ...
}
```

Hmm wait, but existing ids... What about ids with dots or uppercase? The Apps tab lists directories under `apps/`, skipping dot-dirs. Catalog installs — let me check the id format the catalog uses, plus `open_app_tool`. The ticket's security section explicitly says the shared helper validates id `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,31}$`. But if the listing lists a directory that doesn't match this (e.g. `2048 Мини 🎲`?? no — the dir is `2048`, the display name is "2048 Мини 🎲"), then... For list: AC4 says traversal ids (`../x`, absolute) are rejected at list and read. If the listing encounters a dir whose name is invalid (e.g. `..` cannot appear as a dir entry name anyway — well, `listDir` returns entries; a hostile memory env could return `../x` as a name) it must be skipped.

But careful: real user dirs could contain other characters. The catalog install path — let me check `catalog_service.dart` for id validation. Let me also check `apps_catalog_tool.dart` (the agent tool) for id rules.

Also there's the question of where the "root" is: on web, `env.cwd` is '/'; paths like 'apps/2048/manifest.json' are env-relative. `AppsStore` uses relative 'apps/...' paths everywhere; `_AppWidgetFileReader` gets `dir: app.dir` = 'apps/$id'. So the shared helper should produce exactly these paths. The "mount root" on every host is the env itself (cwd '/'). I1 = one helper produces the path for both sides.

Let me check catalog_service for how ids are handled at install time (to make sure the id regex won't reject legitimate catalog ids).

